backups/
*.sql.gz

# v1.26.92 — per-session edit-reminder throttle state, written into the checkout at runtime
state/

# v1.26.105 — the rest of what the installers and hooks write into the checkout at runtime.
# The comment at the top of this file already names the failure: untracked files make
# `git status --porcelain` non-empty, interactive-upgrade reads that as a dirty tree, and
# answers with `git reset --hard`. That list stopped at .update-lock*, so on a real machine
# every upgrade still took the destructive branch — six untracked paths, none of them the
# user's. The warning it prints is therefore noise on every run, which is the expensive part:
# an edit the user actually made scrolls past inside the same message.
.node-path
.git-bash-path
.last-mcp-update-check
# v1.26.143: written by self-check.cjs's usage_roundtrip step. The other markers beside it
# were already ignored; this one was missed, so every self-check left the checkout dirty.
.last-usage-roundtrip
.session-hook-installed
cache/
git-hooks/

# v1.26.143 — two more, found on a real installation two releases later. This list is
# written by hand, which is why it keeps being short by however many paths have been added
# since somebody last looked; that is now caught from the other side, by making untracked
# files stop deciding whether the tree is dirty at all. `git reset --hard` cannot remove an
# untracked file, so a branch chosen because of one can only run again next time.
#
# Only paths OwnMind itself is known to write are listed. A member's machine also reports
# `?? standards/`, and nothing in this repository creates it — so it is the user's, and
# hiding someone's own directory from their own `git status` is not this file's business.
# The change above is what stops it triggering a reset.
bin/
reports/
